Z||000000||sqoach||12-07-2000||09:22 PM||||tightened it, loosened it, tightened it. put some of that belt dressing stuff on it too. still squeals. makes me wonder if a pig is hiding back there.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>it only happens when i turn on the a/c. and i know you'll say to tighten the a/c belt, but it's TIGHT. and it seems like does it a little more when i turn on the lights, which is weird. and the alternator belt is pretty tight too, just don't want to get it too tight. any thoughts?<P>btw, the waterpump a/c belt is new.<P>------------------<BR>sQoaCh<BR>fiero7@hotmail.com<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mysig_2.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||StuGood||12-07-2000||11:45 PM||||sqoach, you should tighten the a/c belt...<P>Dang!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> You were right! I <B>was</B> going to say that  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ! How'd you know  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"> ?!<P>Actually, I was going to recommend that you replace and/tighten the alternator belt.   Do a search through the Forum archives, using "belt" in the subject line, and you'll see there has been a lot of discussion on this very topic.  To summarize, if you want a quiet reliable belt, use good quality, clean, new belt.  Install and adjust it by jacking up the car, and removing the RR tire and inner fender splash shield.  Use the $7 belt tightening tool from Harbor Fright.  It's a little trouble, but it sounds like you're serious about eliminating the squeal.<P>So why do I suspect the alternator belt, when the squealing occurs with the A/C on?   When you turn on the A/C, it automatically turns on the radiator cooling fan up front.  The fan draws considerable power, which increases the load on the alternator belt substantially.  That, along with the fact that squealing is louder with the lights on (which adds to the electrical load), tends to point to the alternator belt.  <P>Good luck  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">! I've been fortunate, in that I've had virtually <B>no</B> belt trouble in any of my Fieros - but as you will read in the archives, it seems to be a very common problem.||||reg||3
Z||000002||sqoach||12-08-2000||01:47 AM||||thanks. i was kinda thinking that, but wasn't sure. i should've gotten both belts instead of just one. show's you how cheap i am  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>i wish i would've had that tightening tool you're talkin about when i tightened the a/c belt. that was a pain in the a$$.<BR>i guess i forgot to mention that that stuff i put on there did help at first, but i keep having to put more on.||||reg||10
Z||000003||Indiana_resto_guy||12-09-2000||12:13 AM||||SqOaCh,<P>If it continues to happen (if your referring to the alt. belt), check the surface of the lower mounting bracket. I had one on an '86 thet had been over-tightened LOTS of times and had gotten to be deformed (not to mention SMOOTH). Went to the parts store and purchased a package of tooth washers and installed one on the adjusting lock down bolt. Problem solved.||||reg||1
Z||000004||sqoach||12-09-2000||01:01 AM||||problem solved  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>well, i can't hear it anymore. i turn as much stuff on as i can and it still does ok. i guess i just had to wait for that stuff i put on it to work. thanks for your help anyway guys.||||reg||1
